      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\), and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\), we will analyze the respective ranges of numbers and count them accordingly.\n\n### Step 1: Count the Number of Four-Digit Odd Numbers \\(A\\)\n\n1. **Range of four-digit numbers**:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\text{ to } 9999\n   \\]\n\n2. **Range of four-digit odd numbers**:\n   \\[\n   1001 \\text{ to } 9999\n   \\]\n\n3. **Counting the four-digit odd numbers**:\n   - The sequence starts at 1001 and ends at 9999.\n   - This is an arithmetic sequence where each term increases by 10 (since we include only odd numbers).\n\n   To find the count of terms in this sequence:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of terms} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{last term} - \\text{first term}}{\\text{common difference}} \\right) + 1 = \\left( \\frac{9999 - 1001}{10} \\right) + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\left( \\frac{8998}{10} \\right) + 1 = 899.8 + 1 = 900.8 \\approx 901\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\) is 901.\n\n### Step 2: Count the Number of Four-Digit Multiples of 5 \\(B\\)\n\n1. **Range of four-digit multiples of 5**:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\text{ to } 9999\n   \\]\n\n2. **Counting the four-digit multiples of 5**:\n   - This sequence starts at 500 (the first four-digit multiple of 5) and continues to 9950 (the largest four-digit multiple of 5).\n\n   To find the count of terms in this sequence:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of terms} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{last term} - \\text{first term}}{\\text{common difference}} \\right) + 1 = \\left( \\frac{9950 - 500}{5} \\right) + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\left( \\frac{9450}{5} \\right) + 1 = 1890 + 1 = 1891\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\) is 1891.\n\n### Step 3: Finding \\(A + B\\)\n\nFinally, we add the quantities obtained for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\):\n\\[\nA + B = 901 + 1891 = 2792\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2792}\n\\]",
